q('W','Mapped types','Key remapping','Define Getters<T> mapping each string key K to get${Capitalize<K>}, with a zero-argument function returning T[K].','type Getters<T> = {\n  [Key in keyof T as Key extends string\n    ? `get${Capitalize<Key>}` : never]: () => T[Key];\n};','Iterate through source keys.~Filter nonstring keys and remap the string name.~Keep the original key when selecting the return type. Name collisions require a deliberate API policy.','Mapped types can rename keys.','Use the original key for the property value type.','ts:handbook/2/mapped-types.html#key-remapping-via-as','// Define Getters<T>.\n// Getters<{ name: string }> has getName: () => string.')
q('M','Conditional types','Disable distribution','Which conditional checks a union as a whole instead of distributing over its members?','[T] extends [string] ? true : false','Wrapping both sides in tuples prevents naked-type-parameter distribution. The whole union must be assignable to string for the true branch.','Hide the naked type parameter.','What tuple wrapper suppresses distribution?','ts:handbook/2/conditional-types.html#distributive-conditional-types',wrong=['T extends string ? true : false','T extends any ? true : false','T extends unknown ? T : never'])
q('C','Functions','Overloads versus unions','When should a function prefer a union parameter over multiple overloads?','Prefer a union when the same operation and return type handle all accepted inputs. Use overloads when distinct call signatures express meaningful input/output relationships. Keep the public API as simple as its contract allows.','Overloads can reject a union-valued argument even when each individual overload accepts one member. More overloads are not automatically more precise for callers.','Look for relationships, not just alternatives.','Does the return type depend on the input shape?','ts:handbook/2/functions.html#writing-good-overloads')
q('B','Optional properties','Exact optional semantics','With exactOptionalPropertyTypes enabled, how can this API explicitly allow assigning undefined as well as omission?','Include undefined explicitly: type Options = { theme?: "dark" | "light" | undefined };','An optional property permits omission.~With exact optional semantics, it does not automatically permit writing undefined unless declared.~Add the union only if explicit undefined is part of the API contract.','Omission differs from a present undefined property.','What values may be assigned when the key exists?','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-4.html#exact-optional-property-types--exactoptionalpropertytypes','type Options = { theme?: "dark" | "light" };\nconst options: Options = { theme: undefined };')
q('W','Generics','Tuple-preserving identity','Write tuple<const T extends readonly unknown[]>(...values: T): T using TypeScript 5.x const type parameters.','function tuple<const T extends readonly unknown[]>(...values: T): T {\n  return values;\n}\n','The const type parameter encourages literal-preserving inference.~The readonly constraint accepts the inferred tuple.~The returned type preserves positional information; this does not freeze the runtime array.','Ask inference to retain literal detail.','Use a readonly-compatible constraint.','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-0.html#const-type-parameters','// Implement tuple so tuple("a", 1) retains literal tuple information.')
q('S','Generics','Correlated union props','A component accepts either { kind: "link", href } or { kind: "button", onClick }. Why is a discriminated union better than making href and onClick optional on one type?','It expresses which fields are required for each mode. Invalid combinations can be rejected statically. Narrowing kind gives the implementation access to the correct fields.','Independent optional properties allow states such as a link with no href. The type should encode the real component contract instead of requiring repeated defensive assumptions.','Model valid combinations.','Which fields depend on the selected mode?','ts:handbook/2/narrowing.html#discriminated-unions')
q('W','Conditional types','Function result inference','Define ResultOf<T> using infer to extract a function return type, and never for nonfunctions.','type ResultOf<T> = T extends (...args: never[]) => infer Result\n  ? Result\n  : never;','The conditional matches function structure.~infer captures the return type.~For overloaded function types, inference uses the last signature rather than performing call-site overload resolution.','Capture the output position.','What happens for an overload set?','ts:handbook/2/conditional-types.html#inferring-within-conditional-types','// Define ResultOf<T> without ReturnType.')
q('M','Utility types','Extract union members','Which type selects only members of Event whose kind is "save"?','Extract<Event, { kind: "save" }>','Extract keeps union members assignable to the supplied shape. Pick selects object properties instead of filtering whole union variants.','Filter variants, not keys.','Which utility performs union selection?','ts:handbook/utility-types.html#extracttype-union',wrong=['Pick<Event, "save">','Partial<Event>','Omit<Event, "kind">'])
q('C','React typing','Nullable DOM refs','Why type a DOM ref as HTMLInputElement | null?','The node is absent before it is attached. It can become absent again when removed. Nullability forces imperative consumers to handle those lifecycle states.','A non-null assertion does not cause a node to exist. Event-time optional access or a suitable guard matches the actual lifecycle.','A ref has an attachment lifecycle.','Can the node be missing at this moment?','react:learn/typescript#typing-useref')
q('U','satisfies','Static versus runtime contracts','Follow-up to TS-021: does satisfies make a downloaded configuration trustworthy at runtime?','No, it is a compile-time check. External data still needs runtime validation. Treat the parsed value as unknown until its required shape is established.','Type-level operators are erased from emitted JavaScript. They cannot inspect data arriving after the program is built.','Ask when the check executes.','Does the operator exist in emitted JavaScript?','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-9.html#the-satisfies-operator')
