q('M','HTML semantics','Action versus navigation','Which element should normally trigger an in-page action such as opening a dialog?','button','A button supplies native activation and keyboard behavior for actions. An anchor represents navigation; a styled div needs behavior and semantics rebuilt.','Choose by behavior, not appearance.','Does the action navigate to a resource?','mdn:Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/button',wrong=['div with only a click handler','span with bold text','a without href solely for its default styling'])
q('B','Forms','Label association','Why is clicking this label not guaranteed to focus the input, and what fixes it?','The for value must match the input id. Use: <input id="email" name="email" type="email">.','The label references an ID, not a name.~The input has no matching ID.~Adding it establishes the relationship; name separately controls submitted form data.','Labels point to IDs.','Is name the same attribute as id?','mdn:Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/label','<label for="email">Email</label>\n<input name="email" type="email">')
q('C','HTML semantics','Landmarks','Why use main, nav and header when their semantics fit the page?','They communicate structural roles beyond visual styling. Assistive technology can use landmarks to navigate sections. Use them according to content purpose rather than as interchangeable layout containers.','Semantic elements do not remove the need for logical headings and accessible controls. Repeating navigation regions may need distinct labels.','Consider navigation without visual layout.','What purpose does each region serve?','mdn:Web/Accessibility/ARIA/Reference/Roles/landmark_role')
q('W','Forms','Required email control','Write a form fragment with a labeled required email input and a submit button using native validation.','<form>\n  <label for="email">Email</label>\n  <input id="email" name="email" type="email" required>\n  <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>\n</form>','The label names the input.~type=email and required provide native constraints.~Submission checks those constraints, but server-side validation is still necessary because client checks can be bypassed.','Use the browser’s form semantics.','Which attributes express presence and format?','mdn:Web/HTML/Guides/Constraint_validation','<!-- Write the form fragment. -->')
q('M','CSS basics','Box sizing','With box-sizing: border-box, what does a declared width include?','Content, padding and border.','border-box includes padding and border within the specified width. Margins remain outside; content-box instead applies width to the content area.','Separate margin from the box.','Which edges are inside the declared width?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/box-sizing',wrong=['Only content.','Content and margin but no border.','The entire viewport width.'])
q('C','CSS basics','Display none and visibility','How do display: none and visibility: hidden differ for layout?','display: none removes the element’s box from layout. visibility: hidden normally retains its layout space while hiding its contents. Choose according to whether surrounding layout should collapse.','Opacity zero is another distinct behavior and can leave interactive content present. Hiding strategies also have accessibility and focus implications.','Ask whether a box still participates.','Should neighboring content move?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/visibility')
q('B','Forms','Implicit submit','Why does this Cancel button submit its surrounding form, and what attribute fixes it?','A button in a form defaults to submit. Use: <button type="button">Cancel</button>.','The missing type selects the submit behavior.~Activating it can submit the form.~Explicit type=button preserves a non-submission action.','Check the default button type.','Is this action meant to submit?','mdn:Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/button#type','<form>\n  <input name="title">\n  <button>Cancel</button>\n</form>')
q('M','CSS layout','Flex axes','For flex-direction: row in a horizontal writing mode, which property distributes items along the main axis?','justify-content','justify-content aligns along the main axis. align-items controls the cross axis, whose physical direction changes with flex-direction and writing mode.','Main and cross axes are logical.','Which property works along the main axis?','mdn:Web/CSS/Guides/Flexible_box_layout/Aligning_items',wrong=['align-items','font-weight','z-index'])
q('W','CSS layout','Basic grid','Write CSS for .cards using three equal columns with a 1rem gap and shrinkable tracks.','.cards {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));\n  gap: 1rem;\n}','Grid creates three flexible tracks.~minmax(0, 1fr) permits tracks to shrink below automatic content minimums.~Content still needs appropriate wrapping or overflow rules if it cannot fit.','Create equal tracks with a zero minimum.','Which function repeats a track definition?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/grid-template-columns','/* Implement .cards. */')
q('C','Responsive design','Mobile-first breakpoints','What does mobile-first CSS usually mean when writing media queries?','Start with styles that work at narrow widths. Add min-width queries for layouts needing more space. Choose breakpoints based on content constraints rather than a fixed list of device models.','Mobile-first does not mean small devices are the only users. The base rules should remain valid when no breakpoint condition matches.','The base style is the fallback.','When does extra space justify a change?','mdn:Web/CSS/Guides/Media_queries/Using')
q('U','Forms','Name versus ID','Follow-up to HCT-002: does adding an id make an input’s value appear in submitted FormData if it has no name?','No, form submission uses the control’s name. The id serves document identification and label association. Add the appropriate name when the control’s value should be submitted.','A visible and well-labeled control can still be absent from submission data. ID and name serve separate contracts.','Submission keys differ from document identifiers.','Which attribute names the submitted field?','mdn:Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/input#name')
q('M','Cascade','Specificity','Within the same origin, importance and cascade layer, which selector has greater specificity?','#profile .name','An ID contributes more specificity than any of the listed class-only alternatives. Cascade origin, importance and layer precedence are considered before specificity.','Compare selector components.','Is there an ID selector?','mdn:Web/CSS/CSS_cascade/Specificity',wrong=['.profile .name','section .name','.name:hover'])
q('B','Flexbox','Automatic minimum size','A flex child containing long text refuses to shrink. Which common CSS fix allows the child to fit, assuming overflow is handled separately?','Set min-width: 0 on the flex child.','A flex item’s automatic minimum can preserve its min-content width.~That minimum can defeat intended shrinking.~A zero minimum allows shrinking; wrapping or overflow handling controls how the text is displayed.','Inspect minimum size, not just flex-shrink.','What lower bound is preventing the shrink?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/min-width',' .row { display: flex; }\n .content { flex: 1; }')
q('C','Grid','auto-fit and auto-fill','How do auto-fit and auto-fill differ when a responsive grid has fewer items than available tracks?','Both can create repeated tracks that fit the container. auto-fit collapses empty repeated tracks. auto-fill preserves those empty tracks, which affects how remaining space is distributed.','The difference becomes visible when there are unused tracks. Both still depend on the chosen minimum and maximum track sizes.','Look at unused tracks.','Are empty tracks kept or collapsed?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Values/repeat')
q('W','Responsive layout','Fluid card grid','Write a .cards grid that fits as many columns as possible, each at least 15rem when space permits, and avoids overflow on narrower containers.','.cards {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr));\n  gap: 1rem;\n}','auto-fit creates as many tracks as fit.~The minimum is capped by available width for narrow containers.~Flexible maxima share remaining space. Intrinsic oversized content may still need wrapping.','Let the container choose the column count.','How can the minimum stay below the container width?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Values/minmax','/* Implement .cards without fixed media-query column counts. */')
q('S','Positioning','Sticky constraints','A sticky sidebar never sticks. Which ancestor and geometry conditions should be checked?','Check that an inset such as top is specified. Inspect which ancestor establishes the scrolling mechanism. Ensure the containing geometry leaves room for the element to move before its containing block ends.','Sticky positioning is constrained by scrolling and containing blocks. A large z-index does not repair a missing inset or unsuitable scroll container.','Find the scrolling ancestor.','Is a sticky inset defined?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/position#sticky')
q('B','Stacking','Local z-index','Why can a tooltip with z-index: 9999 still appear behind another panel?','Its ancestor may be in a lower stacking context. Move the overlay to an appropriate layer or adjust the ancestor stacking order, not just the tooltip’s number.','A stacking context is painted as a unit.~A descendant cannot escape its ancestor’s position in the outer context.~Inspect triggers such as transform, opacity and positioned z-index.','Look above the tooltip.','Which ancestor creates its stacking context?','mdn:Web/CSS/Guides/Positioned_layout/Stacking_context','.panel { transform: translateZ(0); z-index: 1; position: relative; }\n.tooltip { position: absolute; z-index: 9999; }\n.other { position: relative; z-index: 2; }')
q('M','Selectors',':where specificity','What specificity does :where(.card #title) contribute?','Zero.','The :where pseudo-class and its arguments contribute zero specificity. This makes it useful for low-specificity defaults; :is has different specificity behavior.','This pseudo-class deliberately lowers priority.','Do its arguments contribute specificity?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/:where',wrong=['One ID and one class.','One class only.','The same specificity as an inline style.'])
q('C','Forms','Disabled and readonly','How do disabled and readonly inputs differ for form participation?','Disabled controls are generally excluded from submission and normal focus. Readonly supported inputs remain available for focus and submission. Use the attribute that matches whether the value should still participate.','readonly is not supported on every control type. Neither attribute is a server authorization mechanism.','Ask whether the value must be submitted.','Should users still focus and copy it?','mdn:Web/HTML/Reference/Attributes/readonly#attribute_interactions')
q('W','Forms','Validation message lifecycle','Write attachValidation(input) that shows a custom message while value is "admin", clears it otherwise, and returns listener cleanup.','function attachValidation(input) {\n  const validate = () => {\n    input.setCustomValidity(input.value === "admin"\n      ? "Choose a different username." : "");\n  };\n  input.addEventListener("input", validate);\n  validate();\n  return () => input.removeEventListener("input", validate);\n}\n','Set a nonempty custom message only for the forbidden value.~Clear it with an empty string when valid.~Initialize once and return listener cleanup. Leaving an old message would keep the field invalid.','Custom validity persists until cleared.','Which value removes the custom error?','mdn:Web/API/HTMLInputElement/setCustomValidity','// input is an existing HTMLInputElement.\n// Implement attachValidation(input).')
q('M','Tailwind','Responsive variants','In Tailwind v4, what does md:flex mean under the default breakpoint configuration?','Apply display:flex at the md breakpoint and above.','Responsive variants are mobile-first minimum-width conditions by default. They do not mean medium devices only or stop at the next breakpoint automatically.','A breakpoint usually establishes a lower bound.','Is an upper bound included?','tw:responsive-design',wrong=['Apply flex only below md.','Apply flex only between md and lg automatically.','Apply flex only on devices identified as tablets.'])
q('W','Tailwind','Interactive utility variants','Write a Tailwind button with blue background, white text, a darker hover state, and a visible keyboard focus outline.','<button type="button" class="bg-blue-600 px-4 py-2 text-white hover:bg-blue-700 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-blue-600">\n  Save\n</button>','Base utilities define appearance.~hover changes the background during pointer hover.~focus-visible adds a keyboard-appropriate focus indicator. Color changes alone should not replace focus visibility.','State variants prefix utilities.','Which variant targets visible focus?','tw:hover-focus-and-other-states','<!-- Write the button using Tailwind v4 utilities. -->')
q('B','Tailwind','Dynamic class detection','Why might these background classes be missing from the Tailwind build, and what should replace the construction?','The scanner cannot detect complete classes in interpolation. Map to complete strings, for example: const colors = { red: "bg-red-500", blue: "bg-blue-500" };','The source contains fragments rather than complete utility tokens.~Tailwind generates detected class names.~A finite map of complete strings makes both variants discoverable; validate arbitrary external keys.','Detection reads source text.','Does the full class name appear anywhere?','tw:detecting-classes-in-source-files','const className = `bg-${color}-500`;')
q('C','Tailwind','CSS-first theme','How are design tokens commonly configured in Tailwind v4?','Define theme variables with the @theme directive in CSS. Their namespaces generate corresponding utilities. Keep ordinary CSS variables separate when they should not create utility APIs.','A v3 JavaScript configuration file is not the primary v4 configuration pattern. @theme tokens participate in Tailwind’s generated design system.','Use the v4 CSS configuration model.','Should this variable generate a utility?','tw:theme')
q('W','Tailwind','Arbitrary value','Write a Tailwind element whose grid has columns 12rem and the remaining space, using an arbitrary grid-template-columns value.','<div class="grid grid-cols-[12rem_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-4">\n  <aside>Filters</aside>\n  <section>Results</section>\n</div>','The bracket syntax supplies an arbitrary CSS value.~Underscores represent spaces in this utility token.~minmax(0,1fr) lets the flexible column shrink. Arbitrary values should still follow the layout’s responsive needs.','Bracket notation accepts custom values.','How are spaces encoded inside a class token?','tw:adding-custom-styles#using-arbitrary-values','<!-- Write the two-column Tailwind grid. -->')
q('S','CSS units','rem and em','For reusable typography, how do rem and em differ when used as font-size values?','rem refers to the root element’s font size. em in font-size refers to the parent’s computed font size. Nested em-based font sizes can compound while rem remains rooted.','em used on other properties generally follows the element’s own font size. The property context matters, so a single blanket explanation can be misleading.','Check which element supplies the reference.','Is the property font-size or spacing?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Values/length#relative_length_units_based_on_font')
q('B','Responsive design','Fixed overflow','Why can a 700px-wide card overflow a narrow viewport, and what CSS preserves a 700px maximum?','Use a fluid width with a cap: width: 100%; max-width: 700px; box-sizing: border-box;','The fixed width ignores available space.~A percentage width follows the containing block and max-width caps expansion.~border-box keeps padding inside that width; parent constraints still matter.','Use a maximum rather than a fixed requirement.','Can the card shrink with its container?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/max-width','.card {\n  width: 700px;\n  padding: 24px;\n}')
q('C','Selectors','Focus-visible','Why prefer :focus-visible for a custom focus ring instead of simply removing every :focus outline?','It preserves a visible indicator when the browser determines focus should be shown. This supports keyboard navigation without necessarily styling every pointer focus identically. Never remove the default indicator without an adequate replacement.','A visually clean mouse interaction is not evidence that keyboard focus is usable. Focus indication must remain perceivable across themes and states.','Consider keyboard users.','What replaces the removed outline?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/:focus-visible')
q('M','Tailwind','group state','Which pattern lets a child’s text color change when its parent group is hovered?','Add group to the parent and group-hover:text-blue-600 to the child.','group variants refer to an ancestor marked as a group. A plain hover utility on the child responds to the child’s own hover state.','The state belongs to an ancestor.','Which marker identifies that ancestor?','tw:hover-focus-and-other-states#styling-based-on-parent-state',wrong=['Add hover-group to the child only.','Add peer-hover to the parent for every descendant.','Add group-hover to the parent without a child utility.'])
q('W','Tailwind','Dark theme variant','Write a surface using white background and dark text by default, changing to slate-900 and slate-100 with the dark variant.','<section class="bg-white text-slate-900 dark:bg-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n  <h2>Account</h2>\n</section>','Base utilities establish the light appearance.~dark variants apply under the configured dark-mode condition.~By default this follows the system media preference; a custom selector strategy requires configuration.','Use paired base and dark utilities.','What activates dark in this project?','tw:dark-mode','<!-- Use Tailwind v4. Assume the default dark variant. -->')
q('U','Tailwind','Custom token utility','Follow-up to HCT-024: how would a v4 --color-brand-500 theme variable become useful in markup?','Declare it inside @theme. Tailwind can generate utilities such as bg-brand-500 and text-brand-500 from that color token. Keep the token value valid CSS and use complete class names in source.','A variable declared only under :root is an ordinary CSS variable and does not itself create the same utility namespace. Theme registration is the distinction.','The token namespace determines utilities.','Which directive registers the token?','tw:theme#relationship-to-utility-classes')
q('C','Cascade layers','Normal layer precedence','For normal author declarations, how do later named layers compare with earlier named layers and unlayered styles?','Later named layers outrank earlier named layers. Normal unlayered declarations outrank normal layered declarations. Specificity is compared only after the relevant layer precedence is resolved.','A highly specific selector in an earlier layer can lose to a lower-specificity selector in a later layer. Important declarations reverse key layer-priority rules.','Layer order is evaluated before specificity.','Is the declaration normal or important?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@layer')
q('B','Cascade layers','Important layer reversal','Why does the first layer’s important color win here, despite the later layer?','Important declarations reverse named-layer precedence. The earlier base layer’s important declaration outranks theme’s important declaration.','The layer order is base then theme.~Both declarations are important.~For important declarations, earlier layers have higher priority; removing important restores normal later-layer precedence.','Importance changes layer ordering.','Do normal and important declarations share the same layer rule?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@layer#description','@layer base, theme;\n@layer base { .title { color: red !important; } }\n@layer theme { .title { color: blue !important; } }')
q('W','Container queries','Component-width layout','Write CSS that makes .card a two-column grid only when its ancestor .card-container is at least 30rem wide.','.card-container {\n  container-type: inline-size;\n}\n.card {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr;\n}\n@container (min-width: 30rem) {\n  .card { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }\n}','The ancestor establishes an inline-size query container.~The default card uses one track.~The query evaluates the container width, not the viewport. The queried child must be inside the container.','Establish a query container first.','Which width should the component react to?','mdn:Web/CSS/Guides/Containment/Container_queries','/* .card is a child of .card-container. Implement both rules. */')
q('M','Selectors',':has relational matching','Which selector matches a .field that contains an invalid input?','.field:has(input:invalid)','has matches an element based on a relative selector for related content. The result is the field, not the input. Specificity follows its argument rules.','Select the parent based on a descendant.','Which element is before :has?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/:has',wrong=['.field input:invalid','.field::has(input:invalid)','input:invalid > .field'])
q('S','Container queries','Reusable card placement','Why can a container query be a better fit than a viewport media query for a card reused in a sidebar and main column?','The card’s available width can differ at the same viewport size. A container query responds to its local layout context. Keep viewport queries for conditions that genuinely belong to the whole viewport.','A wide viewport does not imply a wide card. Container setup and containment constraints must still be defined on an appropriate ancestor.','The same viewport can contain different widths.','Which dimension governs the card layout?','mdn:Web/CSS/Guides/Containment/Container_queries')
q('W','Tailwind','Custom theme color','Write Tailwind v4 CSS importing Tailwind and registering brand-500 as oklch(0.65 0.15 250).','@import "tailwindcss";\n\n@theme {\n  --color-brand-500: oklch(0.65 0.15 250);\n}\n','The import loads Tailwind’s CSS entry point.~@theme registers the color token.~The color namespace enables utilities such as bg-brand-500. A build integration is still required to process Tailwind directives.','Use the color namespace.','Place the token in the theme directive.','tw:theme#customizing-your-theme','/* Write the Tailwind v4 stylesheet. */')
q('B','Tailwind','v4 import entry','Replace these v3 stylesheet directives with the current v4 import entry point.','Use: @import "tailwindcss";','The old base/components/utilities directives are the v3 setup.~v4 uses the CSS import entry point.~The project must also use its matching v4 build integration, such as the dedicated PostCSS package.','The major version changed CSS setup.','Which package is imported from CSS?','tw:upgrade-guide#removed-tailwind-directives','@tailwind base;\n@tailwind components;\n@tailwind utilities;')
q('C','Layout','Subgrid alignment','What problem does CSS subgrid solve for nested card layouts?','It lets a nested grid use tracks defined by its parent grid. Related content can align across separate nested structures. Choose which axis inherits tracks instead of manually duplicating sizes.','Ordinary nested grids size their tracks independently. Subgrid shares track sizing but does not remove semantic nesting or every layout constraint.','Look for alignment across nested boundaries.','Should the child reuse parent tracks?','mdn:Web/CSS/Guides/Grid_layout/Subgrid')
q('A','CSS architecture','Layered design system','How would you organize reset, base, components and utilities with cascade layers to keep overrides predictable?','Declare the layer order explicitly. Put broad defaults before component styles and intended utility overrides later for normal declarations. Document the use of important because its layer precedence reverses.','Layers reduce specificity battles by expressing priority directly. Leaving unrelated unlayered styles can bypass the intended normal layered order.','Make precedence intentional.','Where do unlayered rules fit?','mdn:Web/CSS/CSS_cascade/Cascade#cascade_layers')
